intro:
This track is about what goes wrong once a program runs on more than one computer, and how to reason about it anyway. Its lessons start from scenes everyone knows. A card reader says "No reply", a friend on a hike stops texting, a video call freezes mid-sentence, a group chat shows an answer above its question, and a microwave blinks 12:00 after a storm. Then they open up the machinery, with small simulations you can run in seconds.

By the end, you can:

- say what a missing reply does and doesn't tell you, and make a retry safe with one key per payment;
- pick a timeout from measured delays, and spot a machine that every health check calls fine while customers see errors;
- pick the right clock for measuring a duration and for stamping a time, and say how far a synced clock can be off;
- explain why a program can freeze for seconds without noticing, and make the thing it writes to refuse a late write;
- put events in cause-and-effect order without trusting any clock;
- name the faults an algorithm is proved against, and tell a promise that nothing bad happens from one that something good will;
- test a distributed design in layers, from a model checker to a seeded simulator that replays any failure exactly.

The eight lessons:

1. Partial failure: networks that lose, delay and duplicate
2. Detecting failure: timeouts, heartbeats and gray failure
3. Clocks you can't trust: wall clocks, monotonic clocks and NTP
4. Process pauses: when your own program stops the world
5. Leases, locks and fencing tokens
6. Ordering without a shared clock: logical clocks and IDs
7. System models: crashes, lies, safety and liveness
8. Testing distributed systems: Jepsen, TLA+ and simulation

You need to read a little code, in Kotlin or Java. The Replication track (one computer takes the changes, the others copy them) and the Transactions track (groups of changes that succeed or fail together) come first. Every term is explained again, though, the first time a lesson uses it. The Consistency and consensus track comes next and builds on lessons 5 to 8: its rules for what a read may return, and its election algorithm, Raft, assume the faults this track sets up.

Seven of the eight labs are plain Kotlin simulations in the `sim` module. Each takes every random choice from one number, the seed, and runs on a pretend clock. So a run with crashes, pauses and network splits takes milliseconds, and comes out the same every time. The tests use JUnit and jqwik. Lesson 5's lab takes a real lock in Redis, a program that keeps data in memory for many programs at once. So it needs Docker, a free program that runs small, separate systems called containers on your computer. Run a lab from the `labs` folder with `./gradlew :sim:test --tests 'sfz.dist.lNN.*'` (lesson 5: `./gradlew :redis:test --tests 'sfz.dist.l05.*'`).
